{
  "big_picture": {
    "problem": [
      {
        "text": "A question-answering system fetches a pile of passages and then reads them all as if each were equally trustworthy, so a few off-topic passages can drown out the one that holds the answer. ",
        "cites": [
          "ch01/p002"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The paper's maths does one job: give every passage a trust score, then let the reader listen to each passage in proportion to that score.",
        "cites": [
          "ch03/s01/p001",
          "ch03/s01/p003"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "map": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "direction": "LR",
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": "inputs",
          "label": "Question q and passage p_i"
        },
        {
          "id": "gate",
          "label": "Gate score g_i (formula 1)"
        },
        {
          "id": "attn",
          "label": "Re-weighted attention (formula 2)"
        },
        {
          "id": "answer",
          "label": "The answer: +2.1 exact match"
        }
      ],
      "edges": [
        {
          "from": "inputs",
          "to": "gate",
          "label": "compared"
        },
        {
          "from": "gate",
          "to": "attn",
          "label": "scales"
        },
        {
          "from": "attn",
          "to": "answer",
          "label": "reads"
        }
      ],
      "cites": [
        "ch03/s01/m001",
        "ch03/s01/m002",
        "ch04/s02/p001"
      ]
    }
  },
  "formulas": [
    {
      "anchor": "ch03/s01/m001",
      "latex": "g_i = \\sigma(w^\\top [q; p_i])",
      "name": "The trust score",
      "in_plain_words": [
        {
          "text": "The trust score of passage i equals: glue the question and the passage together, turn them into one number with a set of learned weights, then squash that number so it lands between 0 and 1.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/m001",
            "ch03/s01/p002"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "symbols": [
        {
          "symbol": "g_i",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "The trust score of passage number i: 0 means ignore it, 1 means trust it fully.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p002"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "symbol": "q",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "The question, written as a list of numbers the computer can compare.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p001"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "symbol": "p_i",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "Passage number i, also written as a list of numbers.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p001"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "symbol": "w",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "A set of volume knobs, one per number, that the system tunes while it learns.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p002"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "symbol": "\\sigma",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "A squashing machine: whatever number goes in, a number between 0 and 1 comes out.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p002"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "intuition": [
        {
          "text": "Think of a judge with a scoring sheet: each line of the sheet (a knob in w) says how much one feature of the question-and-passage pair matters. Adding up the weighted lines gives a raw score; the squashing machine turns it into a percentage, so scores are always comparable.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/m001",
            "ch03/s01/p002"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "diagram": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "direction": "LR",
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "pair",
            "label": "Question and passage side by side"
          },
          {
            "id": "dot",
            "label": "Weigh each number and add up"
          },
          {
            "id": "squash",
            "label": "Squash into 0 to 1"
          },
          {
            "id": "score",
            "label": "Trust score g_i"
          }
        ],
        "edges": [
          {
            "from": "pair",
            "to": "dot"
          },
          {
            "from": "dot",
            "to": "squash"
          },
          {
            "from": "squash",
            "to": "score"
          }
        ],
        "cites": [
          "ch03/s01/m001",
          "ch03/s01/p002"
        ]
      },
      "worked_example": {
        "setup": [
          {
            "text": "Pretend the glued question-and-passage is just two numbers, 1 and 2, and the knobs are 0.5 and 1.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m001"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "steps": {
          "type": "steps",
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Weigh each number by its knob and add: 0.5 × 1 + 1 × 2 = 2.5.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Squash it: σ(2.5) = 1 / (1 + e^(−2.5)), and e^(−2.5) is about 0.08.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001",
                  "ch03/s01/p002"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "So the score is 1 / (1 + 0.08) ≈ 0.93.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        "result": [
          {
            "text": "A trust score of about 0.93: this passage will count for a lot when the reader writes the answer.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m001",
              "ch03/s01/p003"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "extra": {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "The squashing machine at three raw scores",
          "header": [
            "Raw score",
            "Trust score"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              {
                "text": "−2",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              },
              {
                "text": "0.12",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "0",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              },
              {
                "text": "0.5",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "2",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              },
              {
                "text": "0.88",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m001"
                ]
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "why_here": [
        {
          "text": "The reader needs a number per passage that says how relevant it looks before reading it closely; the gate is where that number comes from.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/p001",
            "ch03/s01/p002"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "added_value": [
        {
          "text": "Without the gate every passage counts the same, which is exactly the failure the paper starts from: irrelevant passages dilute the evidence.",
          "cites": [
            "ch01/p002",
            "ch03/s01/p003"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "common_confusion": {
        "type": "callout",
        "tone": "caution",
        "title": "A score is not a probability of being right",
        "spans": [
          {
            "text": "A trust score of 0.9 does not mean the passage is correct 90% of the time; it only says how much the reader should listen to it.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/p002",
              "ch03/s01/p003"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "try_it": {
        "question": [
          {
            "text": "If the raw score is exactly 0, what trust score comes out?",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m001"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          {
            "text": "σ(0) = 1 / (1 + e^0) = 1 / (1 + 1) = 0.5: an undecided, middle score.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m001"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "explore_further": {
        "prerequisites": [
          {
            "concept": "Dot product",
            "shelf_concept": null
          },
          {
            "concept": "Sigmoid function",
            "shelf_concept": null
          }
        ],
        "search_terms": [
          "dot product intuition",
          "logistic sigmoid"
        ],
        "next_question": [
          {
            "text": "Why squash the score at all, instead of using the raw number directly?",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m001",
              "ch03/s01/p002"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "anchor": "ch03/s01/m002",
      "latex": "\\alpha_i' = \\frac{g_i \\alpha_i}{\\sum_j g_j \\alpha_j}",
      "name": "Listening in proportion to trust",
      "in_plain_words": [
        {
          "text": "The new share of attention for passage i is its old share multiplied by its trust score, divided by the same product added up over every passage, so all the shares still add up to one.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/m002",
            "ch03/s01/p003"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "symbols": [
        {
          "symbol": "\\alpha_i",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "How much of its attention the reader already gave passage i, as a share of the whole.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p003"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "symbol": "\\alpha_i'",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "That share after the trust scores have had their say.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p003"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "symbol": "g_i",
          "everyday": [
            {
              "text": "The trust score from the first formula.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/m002"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "intuition": [
        {
          "text": "Like splitting a pizza: everyone's slice is scaled by how hungry they are, and dividing by the total makes sure the slices still add up to exactly one pizza.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/m002",
            "ch03/s01/p003"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "diagram": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "direction": "LR",
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "old",
            "label": "Old attention share"
          },
          {
            "id": "trust",
            "label": "Trust score"
          },
          {
            "id": "times",
            "label": "Multiply"
          },
          {
            "id": "total",
            "label": "Divide by the total"
          },
          {
            "id": "new",
            "label": "New share"
          }
        ],
        "edges": [
          {
            "from": "old",
            "to": "times"
          },
          {
            "from": "trust",
            "to": "times"
          },
          {
            "from": "times",
            "to": "total"
          },
          {
            "from": "total",
            "to": "new"
          }
        ],
        "cites": [
          "ch03/s01/m002"
        ]
      },
      "worked_example": {
        "setup": [
          {
            "text": "Two passages share the reader's attention half and half, and their trust scores are 0.9 and 0.1.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m002"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "steps": {
          "type": "steps",
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Multiply each share by its trust: 0.9 × 0.5 = 0.45 and 0.1 × 0.5 = 0.05.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m002"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Add them up: 0.45 + 0.05 = 0.5.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m002"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Divide each by the total: 0.45 / 0.5 = 0.9 and 0.05 / 0.5 = 0.1.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch03/s01/m002"
                ]
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        "result": [
          {
            "text": "The trusted passage now gets 90% of the attention and the doubtful one 10%, instead of half each.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m002",
              "ch03/s01/p003"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "extra": null
      },
      "why_here": [
        {
          "text": "This is where the trust score actually changes what the reader does: it rescales the attention the reader pays to each passage.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/p003"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "added_value": [
        {
          "text": "Multiplying without dividing by the total would let the shares add up to less than one, shrinking the reader's attention overall; the division keeps it a fair split.",
          "cites": [
            "ch03/s01/m002"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "common_confusion": {
        "type": "callout",
        "tone": "caution",
        "title": "The sum runs over every passage",
        "spans": [
          {
            "text": "The j under the big sigma means 'every passage, one after another', not one particular passage.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m002"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "try_it": {
        "question": [
          {
            "text": "With shares 0.5 and 0.5 and trust scores 0.6 and 0.2, what new share does the first passage get?",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m002"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          {
            "text": "0.6 × 0.5 = 0.3 and 0.2 × 0.5 = 0.1; the total is 0.3 + 0.1 = 0.4, so the first passage gets 0.3 / 0.4 = 0.75.",
            "cites": [
              "ch03/s01/m002"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "explore_further": {
        "prerequisites": [
